PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §4.1: "أضف migration runner: backup قابل للتحقق،
migration idempotent، نسخة schema، rollback/restore مجرّب، وفتح نسخة
أحدث/أقدم بسلوك صريح."

Added internal/store/migrate.go, wired into OpenSQLite before the existing
CREATE TABLE/INDEX IF NOT EXISTS schemaDDL - which is safe only for additive
changes and would otherwise silently leave an old structure in place for
anything a real future migration needs to restructure.

Behaviour, each one covered by a real test in migrate_test.go:
- A brand-new database: no-op, no backup file created.
- A database already at the current schema version: no-op, idempotent
  (re-running it a second time does not re-run any migration step).
- A database at a newer version than the running binary understands:
  refused outright, file untouched, explicit error naming both versions.
- A database behind the current version with no registered path forward:
  refused, file untouched, explicit error naming the gap.
- A database behind the current version WITH a registered path: WAL is
  checkpointed, a verified backup (opened read-only and checked with SQLite's
  own PRAGMA integrity_check) is taken before anything is touched, every step
  runs inside one transaction, and only commits (bumping the recorded
  version) if every step succeeded.
- A migration step that fails partway: the whole transaction rolls back, the
  file is provably unchanged (asserted directly against the data a step
  partially wrote before failing), and the error names the verified backup's
  path.
- RestoreBackup: copies a backup over a target, verifying both the source and
  the restored copy before touching the target, replacing it with a single
  rename (never a partially-written file). Tested both for a real round trip
  and for refusing a corrupt backup outright.

No real migration exists yet - event.SchemaVersion has only ever been 1, so
there is nothing to migrate from. The runner is proven with a synthetic
migration registered only inside the test (withMigrations), specifically so
the mechanism is trustworthy before it is ever needed for real, per the
plan's own instruction not to start capabilities the code cannot yet prove.

Verification: full test suite (Windows and Linux, the latter with -race)
clean; the real 14-scenario lab gate (lab/inject.sh all) re-run against
binaries built with this change: PASS, identical to every prior run - the
migration check runs on every single store open and adds no observable
change to normal operation, which is exactly what it should do when there is
nothing to migrate.
